1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,210
Hi, I'm Anmol Adwani. This is my walkthrough for the Voice Arena take-home.

2
00:00:05,510 --> 00:00:09,053
I designed a public leaderboard for speaker diarization,

3
00:00:09,053 --> 00:00:12,660
who spoke when, and tested my own choices on real audio.

4
00:00:13,310 --> 00:00:15,760
The board is for teams picking a system:

5
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:18,700
meeting notes, call analytics, medical scribes.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,593
Their question isn't who's number one.

7
00:00:21,593 --> 00:00:25,550
It's which system works on my audio, and is the gap real?

8
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:30,350
So the hidden test set covers seven domains, and each one counts equally.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,759
To test my choices, I ran a small version:

10
00:00:33,759 --> 00:00:39,540
three open pipelines on three AMI meetings, close and far mics, fifty-four scored runs.

11
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:44,250
Choice one is strict scoring: no collar, and overlapping speech counts.

12
00:00:44,550 --> 00:00:46,843
The lenient settings many papers use dropped

13
00:00:46,843 --> 00:00:49,240
forty-one percent of the speech from scoring.

14
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:52,952
Choice two: systems aren't told the speaker count,

15
00:00:52,952 --> 00:00:56,810
and every open model gets the same tuning on a public dev set.

16
00:00:57,110 --> 00:01:01,660
On defaults, one model guessed fifty-nine speakers in a four-person meeting.

17
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:06,101
After tuning, its error fell from sixty-one to twenty-six percent,

18
00:01:06,101 --> 00:01:07,670
and the ranking flipped.

19
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:11,990
Choice three: DER is never shown alone.

20
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:16,530
In one clip, one person spoke ninety-seven percent of the time.

21
00:01:16,530 --> 00:01:22,790
Merging everyone into one speaker scored fifteen percent DER, but seventy-eight percent JER.

22
00:01:23,090 --> 00:01:24,690
So JER sits beside it,

23
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:29,270
and close systems share a tier, decided by a paired bootstrap.

24
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:32,990
As a product, domain is the first filter.

25
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:37,610
Every score shows its confidence interval and error breakdown,

26
00:01:37,610 --> 00:01:41,790
and APIs that can't output overlap are flagged, not hidden.

27
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:44,810
DIHARD Three shaped my thinking most.

28
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:49,870
My lesson: a diarization score means nothing without its settings and domain.

29
00:01:49,870 --> 00:01:52,590
The code is on GitHub. Thanks for watching!

